  <h2>What is gin?</h2>
  <p>In a nutshell, gin is a distilled spirit that gets its flavour from juniper berries.</p>
  <p>It was brought over to Britain by European visitors, most notably the Dutch, as a medicinal drink, and by 1688 it had become the nation’s favourite. The British adapted it to be a bit more of a… social drink. Classic Britain.</p>

  <h2>Popular gin cocktails</h2>
  <p>The most iconic gin cocktail of all time is the Gin and Tonic. It doesn’t get much better than that, and it works with any flavoured gin that comes along.</p>
  <p>Other classic gin cocktails include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/tom-collins/">Tom Collins</a>, the offshoot of the <a href="/cocktails/john-collins/">John Collins</a>. It mixes Old Tom gin, lemon juice, sugar syrup and tonic water.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/negroni/">Negroni</a>, a classic mix of gin, sweet vermouth and <a href="/what-is-campari/">Campari</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/french-75/">French 75</a>, a classy drink that mixes gin, champagne, lemon juice and simple syrup.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/singapore-sling/">Singapore Sling</a>, a classic, boozy cocktail that contains ALL THE ALCOHOL. Including gin.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/fluffy-duck/">Fluffy Duck</a>, a smooth cocktail that combines gin, advocaat, triple sec, orange juice and soda water.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/long-island-iced-tea/">Long Island Iced Tea</a>, another cocktail that contains ALL THE ALCOHOL. It’s a classic that’ll mess you up good.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What gin should I use?</h2>
  <p>Whatever gin you have in the house is great for most of these cocktails. But for gin and tonics, or if you’re in the market for a new bottle, we recommend Hendrick’s.</p>

